    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** standing out as the unequivocal anchor of our offensive strategy, boasting an elite 81.26 efficiency score against Hunter Dobbins. The combination of a favorable R/R platoon matchup and the premier leadoff spot maximizes his opportunity to generate the stolen bases we desperately need to stay competitive in that category. Alongside him, **Lars Nootbaar** (67.77) and **Yordan Alvarez** (65.52) provide significant firepower. **Lars Nootbaar** benefits from a massive 11% boost due to his platoon positioning and leadoff role, while **Yordan Alvarez** remains a must-start despite a slightly tougher matchup against Bryan Woo, as his elite .465 xwOBA effectively renders traditional pitching matchups secondary.\n\nWhile our starting lineup is robust, the rotation decision regarding our bench was dictated by the fine margins of our scoring thresholds. **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** (50.86) was the most difficult exclusion; he finished just behind the marginal starts of **Kazuma Okamoto** and **Colt Keith**. The optimizer ultimately sidelined **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** due to a suboptimal batting order projection (#6) and a lack of elite sprint speed compared to our infield starters, which is a critical weighting factor for our 5x5 Roto goals. Meanwhile, the biggest risk on our roster today is **Christian Yelich**, whose score of 39.02 falls below the efficiency floor. Facing the elite arm of Tarik Skubal (2.77 xERA), **Christian Yelich** is relegated to the bench to preserve our game caps, as the difficulty of the matchup severely limits his potential for offensive contribution. \n\nManagers should remain vigilant regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status across the board. While we have projected **Gary Sanchez** and **Trea Turner** based on their historical roles, any shift in the lineup order could dampen their efficiency scores, particularly for **Trea Turner**, whose 58.52 score relies heavily on his #2 spot. We are avoiding exposure to top-tier arms where possible; **Gary Sanchez** is a volatile play at 42.07 against Skubal, but we are effectively capped by his position. Ensure that if any of our starters\u2014specifically **Junior Caminero** or **Carson Benge**\u2014are moved down the lineup, we reassess their start status immediately.",
